From: doug@OpenMind.com (Doug Cutrell)
Date: Sat, 17 Sep 1994 19:27:28 -0700
Subject: [#94-9-219] Liquid Sentience

I have been reading the list for several months now, and have observed many
discussions about possible directions for the evolution of human
consciousness, AI, or some sort of synergism between them.  However, I have
been surprised to find that no one has mentioned what seems to me to be an
obvious possibility... that the notion of sentience will cease to have
meaning in terms of discrete entities, but rather be described as a
continuum or liquid.

It is difficult to find meaningful language to discuss this notion, which
is perhaps why I haven't seen any discussion of it.  Even so, I'd like to
describe my conception of it in case other's may be interested in the idea.
I apologize if this rehashes old ideas!

My observation is that throughout the discussions of "uploads",
intelligence augmentation, AIs, etc. there is an underlying reliance on
some sort of meaning for the term "individual".  Even when people try to
discuss "group minds", they tend to discuss these as a collective of
individuals, wherein perhaps the sum is greater than the parts, but the
parts still retain some sort of meaning *as* parts.

Certainly our biological past and the grammatical structure of our language
have a lot to do with this assumption -- but I don't see any a priori
reason why this notion of *individuality* would necessarily be retained in
the future.  It seems to derive from our biological roots as sentience
evolving in separate, non-miscible physical bodies and minds.  However, as
soon as it is possible to meaningfully discuss the possibility of
"uploads", or of transferring our consciousness to "artificial brains", the
need for individuality seems to vanish.

I would propose that we could meaningfully talk about sentience as a fluid
substance.  Sentience in this sense might be fully *miscible* with other
sentience... the result of combining separate portions of sentience is
simply a new, larger portion of sentience, and all traces of any identity
to the previous components is totally gone.  Likewise, sentience might
spontaneously break up into many pieces, none of which had any identity or
meaning prior to the decomposition.  One might speak of "virtual"
individuals, present in potentiality but ever effervescent, spontaneously
forming and recombining in endless variety.

For a discussion of this scenario, most of the language that we use to
discuss possible future developments become useless.  For example, instead
of "reproducing", sentience can simply *spread*.  All references to self
("I", "you", "them") must be questioned.

Further abstractions may be possible... I imagine that fluid sentience
still retains a notion of "cohesiveness", such that a given quantity of
sentience has a sense of "measure", regardless of how many pieces it has
divided or recombined into (the term "quantity" in this case begs the
question, in fact).  But one might imagine further metaphors -- perhaps
sentience as a "gas", which would not share this constraint.  However, it
certainly becomes increasingly difficult to find anything meaningful that
can be said with our language.

Doug Cutrell

