Right.  And it's that weakness of his that drives most of the stories involving
him.  It was the Beyonder's lack of any such weakness that made the stories 
about him so boring - that, and the inconsistency of a supposedly omnipotent
being being ignorant about what being human is like.

>[comments on X-Men and Captain America deleted.  Yes, X-Men has
>some very good philosophical points. It's funny, how adult censors
>complain about the X-Men animated series so much for being nothing
>but "violence", yet the story has a consistent moral theme with
>righteous characters. Especially Xavier, Storm, and Cyclops. 
>The people complaining about x-men too often focus on the character
>of Wolverine. The cartoon is also heavily watered down from the
>original comic.]

It seems that the kind of violence that gets complained about most
is righteous violence, the kind with a moral justification.  In a
recent issue of Sharon Presley's newsletter, there was an article by
a psychologist about TV violence which mentioned how kids are more
likely to be violent after they've seen violence on TV portrayed as
justified - like justifiable homicide!  This "all violence is bad"
stuff is a positive menace.  J. Neil Schulman makes this point well
in his book "Stopping Power": the fifth Commandment isn't really
"thou shalt not kill," it's "thou shalt not _murder_."  It also
says that there is a time to kill, such as in self-defense when all
other options have been exhausted.

Such pacifism can all too easily become quietism, the notion that we
mustn't resist our oppressors because that would be immoral.

Of course, the notion that the X-men animated series is "too violent"
is silly.  Even the comic book isn't that violent.  Try "The Punisher,"
or "Judge Dredd."  But even this is beside the point.  The real point
is that there is a human need to confront violence, even death, in
a physically safe way.  Art is one way to do that.

